Q: Resetting offset for partition does not work A: <p>I can't reproduce your issue.</p>
<p>Here is my test Spring Boot application:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@SpringBootApplication
public class So62465345Application extends AbstractConsumerSeekAware {


    private static final Logger LOG = LoggerFactory.getLogger(So62465345Application.class);


    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(So62465345Application.class, args);
    }

    @KafkaListener(id = &quot;so62465345&quot;, topics = &quot;so62465345&quot;)
    public void listen(String in) {
        System.out.println(in);
    }

    @Bean
    public NewTopic topic() {
        return TopicBuilder.name(&quot;so62465345&quot;).partitions(10).replicas(1).build();
    }

    @Bean
    public ApplicationRunner runner(KafkaTemplate&lt;String, String&gt; template) {
        return args -&gt; IntStream.range(0, 9).forEach(i -&gt; template.send(&quot;so62465345&quot;, i, null,
                System.currentTimeMillis() + &quot;:foo:&quot; + i));
    }

    @Override
    public void onPartitionsAssigned(Map&lt;TopicPartition, Long&gt; assignments, ConsumerSeekCallback callback) {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000);
        }
        catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
        LOG.info(&quot;Seeking on assignment&quot;);
        callback.seekToBeginning(assignments.keySet());
    }

    @Override
    public void onIdleContainer(Map&lt;TopicPartition, Long&gt; assignments, ConsumerSeekCallback callback) {
        LOG.info(&quot;Seeking on idle&quot;);
        callback.seekToBeginning(assignments.keySet());
    }

}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>spring.kafka.consumer.auto-offset-reset=earliest
spring.kafka.listener.idle-event-interval=30000
spring.kafka.listener.poll-timeout=2000
</code></pre>
<p>I have set a breakpoint in the <code>onIdleContainer</code> and, using <code>kafka-console-consumer</code>, I see that the offsets are not actually reset until the next <code>poll()</code>.</p>
<p><code>Seeking to EARLIEST offset of partition so62465345-1</code> appears when we perform the seek, but the <code>Resetting offset for partition so62465345-0 to offset 0</code> doesn't appear until we call <code>poll()</code> again (and then the offsets are actually reset).</p>
<p>So I do see that the seeks don't occur on the current poll, which returns 0 records, but the next poll starts from the beginning.</p>

Q: How to simplify checking whether a reference variable is null? A: <p>If you can use C# 14's new <code>extension</code> syntax, then you can do this:</p>
<pre><code>public static class MyExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;( ref T self )
        where T : struct
    {
        public Boolean TryDeref( out T value )
        {
            if( Unsafe.IsNullRef( in self ) )
            {
                value = default;
                return false;
            }
            else
            {
                value = self;
                return true;
            }

        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>...used like so:</p>
<pre><code>
// Based on this `ref return` example: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/statements/jump-statements#ref-returns

public static void Main()
{
    Int32[] xs = new Int32[] { 10, 20, 30, 40 };
    ref Int32 found = ref FindFirst( xs, s =&gt; s == 30 );
    found = 0;

    Console.WriteLine( String.Join( &quot; &quot;, xs ) );  // output: 10 20 0 40

    if( found.TryDeref( out Int32 ok ) )
    {
        Console.WriteLine( ok );
    }
}

private static ref Int32 FindFirst( Int32 [] numbers, Func&lt;Int32,Boolean&gt; predicate )
{
    for( Int32 i = 0; i &lt; numbers.Length; i++ )
    {
        if( predicate( numbers[i] ) )
        {
            return ref numbers[i];
        }
    }

    throw new InvalidOperationException( &quot;No element satisfies the given condition.&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>However, I don't really see any advantage in using the <code>TryGet( out T value )</code>-convention because <code>ref T</code> behaves just like a <code>T</code> provided you don't mutate it, in which case an extension-property is simpler:</p>
<pre><code>public static class MyExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;( ref T self )
        where T : struct
    {
        public Boolean IsOK =&gt; !Unsafe.IsNullRef( in self )
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage:</p>
<pre><code>public static void Main()
{
    Int32[] xs = new Int32[] { 10, 20, 30, 40 };
    ref Int32 found = ref FindFirst( xs, s =&gt; s == 30 );
    found = 0;

    Console.WriteLine( String.Join( &quot; &quot;, xs ) );  // output: 10 20 0 40

    if( found.IsOK )
    {
        Console.WriteLine( found );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Faffing around with generic extensions - <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/docs/blob/0f663b007ca51ed742b5f3416145de67ca9f0121/docs/csharp/language-reference/compiler-messages/extension-declarations.md?plain=1#L127" rel="nofollow noreferrer">and new C# 14 CS93xx errors</a>:</h2>
<p>As of 2026-02-19, the definition of <code>Unsafe.IsNullRef</code> <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/f84f00920e1719071492b260375af0d4403d340e/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Runtime/CompilerServices/Unsafe.cs#L792" rel="nofollow noreferrer">is as-follows</a>:</p>
<pre><code>// CoreCLR:METHOD__UNSAFE__BYREF_IS_NULL
// AOT: IsNullRef
[Intrinsic]
[NonVersionable]
[MethodImpl( AggressiveInlining )]
public static Boolean IsNullRef&lt;T&gt;( ref readonly T source )
    where T : allows ref struct
{
    return AsPointer( in source ) == null;

    // ldarg.0
    // ldc.i4.0
    // conv.u
    // ceq
    // ret
}
</code></pre>
<p>Interestingly, <code>AsPointer</code> is not defined in C#.</p>
<p>Anyway, I initially based my <code>extension</code> on the same generic type constraints as <code>Unsafe.IsNullRef</code>, i.e. <code>&lt;T&gt; where T : allows ref struct</code> (documented here), but it gave me an error for my use of <code>T</code> in <code>extension&lt;T&gt;( ref readonly T self )</code>:</p>
<pre><code>public static class MyExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;( ref readonly T self ) // #ERROR: CS9301 The 'in' or 'ref readonly' receiver parameter of extension must be a concrete (non-generic) value type.
        where T : allows ref struct
    {
        public Boolean IsOK =&gt; !Unsafe.IsNullRef( in self );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>CS9301 The 'in' or 'ref readonly' receiver parameter of extension must be a concrete (non-generic) value type.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>Similarly, <code>ref T self</code> also errored-out:</p>
<pre><code>public static class MyExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;( ref readonly T self ) // #ERROR: CS9300 The 'ref' receiver parameter of an extension block must be a value type or a generic type constrained to struct.
        where T : allows ref struct
    {
        public Boolean IsOK =&gt; !Unsafe.IsNullRef( in self );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>CS9300 The 'ref' receiver parameter of an extension block must be a value type or a generic type constrained to struct.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>...but changing the constraint to <code>where T : struct</code> also fails:</p>
<pre><code>public static class MyExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;( ref readonly T self ) // #ERROR: CS9301 The 'in' or 'ref readonly' receiver parameter of extension must be a concrete (non-generic) value type.
        where T : struct
    {
        public Boolean IsOK =&gt; !Unsafe.IsNullRef( in self );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>CS9301 The 'in' or 'ref readonly' receiver parameter of extension must be a concrete (non-generic) value type.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>...but removing <code>readonly</code> makes it work:</p>
<pre><code>public static class MyExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;( ref T self )
        where T : struct
    {
        public Boolean IsOK =&gt; !Unsafe.IsNullRef( in self );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I'll admit I don't yet understand why (I still haven't yet used C# 14 for any real work yet, as I'm still on .NET 4.8 <em>*le sigh*</em>)</p>

Q: On Duplicate Key Update same as insert A: <pre><code>//To update by readding values on duplicate
INSERT INTO table_name (column1, column2, key)
VALUES (value1, value2, key_value)
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE
column1 = value1,
column2 = value2;

//To update without re adding values, delete old values
DELETE FROM table_name WHERE key = key_value;
//Then re insert fresh values
INSERT INTO table_name (column1, column2, key)
VALUES (value1, value2, key_value);
</code></pre>

Q: Add a text above an image using mudbalzor A: <p>I've modified the great answer by Bart to use <code>MudOverlay</code> instead of the <code>MudPaper</code> component:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>@inherits MudComponentBase

&lt;div style=&quot;position: relative; display: inline-block;&quot;&gt;
    &lt;MudImage @attributes=&quot;base.UserAttributes&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;MudOverlay Visible DarkBackground Absolute Style=&quot;top: initial; bottom: 0; height: auto; justify-content: flex-start; padding: 25px;&quot;&gt;
        @ChildContent
    &lt;/MudOverlay&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

@code {
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent  { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then it would be used in the parent as follows:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;CustomImage Src=&quot;images/hamburger.jpg&quot;&gt;
    &lt;MudText Typo=&quot;Typo.h4&quot;&gt;A tasty burger&lt;/MudText&gt;
    &lt;MudText Typo=&quot;Typo.h6&quot;&gt;Some more text or a price...&lt;/MudText&gt;
&lt;/CustomImage&gt;
</code></pre>
